00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:37.840
As we observe the world around us, what we see&nbsp;
is shaped by the way our eyes process the light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:44.720
that’s bouncing and absorbing and passing&nbsp;
through our surroundings. For many of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:53.120
what this means is that when we look at this clip&nbsp;
of cyanobacteria, we see green strips sliding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:00.720
along a blue background. For others, depending on&nbsp;
their eyes and any other corrective measures, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:07.920
colors may be different, or the edges blurred, or&nbsp;
the image may not exist at all. The final product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.960
will come down to the physical and chemical&nbsp;
reactions that are taking place in our eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:22.880
in response to light, and how that informs&nbsp;
the biological processing that comes next.&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:31.840
Microbes don’t have this sort of complex visual&nbsp;
processing, but many still need to know where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.240
the light around them is--whether that’s&nbsp;
to seek out food or avoid hidden dangers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.680
They may not see things the way we do,&nbsp;
but the mechanisms they have in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.560
allow them to respond to light in extraordinary&nbsp;
ways to meet the most ordinary of needs.&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:58.080
Perhaps the most primitive of these methods are&nbsp;
light-sensitive pigments called phytochromes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.360
But when we say “primitive,” we don’t&nbsp;
mean to diminish their importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:10.000
or their complexity. If whole ecosystems are&nbsp;
built on photosynthesis, then phytochromes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.960
are essential to life as it currently exists.
Scientists first discovered phytochromes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.360
plants, where they helped answer longstanding&nbsp;
questions about how plants respond to seasonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.760
changes in light. But the ancestors of those&nbsp;
plant pigments are found in cyanobacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:36.320
like oscillatoria, whose phytochromes help&nbsp;
regulate growth, movement, and photosynthesis.&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:43.200
These phytochromes respond not just to the&nbsp;
amount of light, but also the color of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:50.320
And remarkably, cyanobacteria phytochromes&nbsp;
can respond to a wider range of colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.320
than plant phytochromes, which&nbsp;
makes sense if we think about where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:03.040
cyanobacteria live. They live in water.
Because of how water absorbs light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:09.120
there’s usually more red light at the surface&nbsp;
and then more green and blue light the deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:16.880
you go. If a cyanobacteria were to have a pigment&nbsp;
that detects only red light or only blue light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:24.320
it would end up restricted in where it could live.
So instead, these simple little organisms perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:31.680
a complex calculation called chromatic&nbsp;
adaptation, calibrating the amount of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.680
various pigments so they can maximize how much&nbsp;
light they’re getting from their environment.&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:45.840
But cyanobacteria are unicellular organisms&nbsp;
that want light. What about those that don’t?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:54.240
Both blepharisma and Stentor coeruleus are&nbsp;
well-known for their photophobic nature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:01.280
The reaction is straightforward: when confronted&nbsp;
with light, these microbes start backpedaling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
This response is set off when light&nbsp;
interacts with their pigments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.920
the pink blepharismin in the blepharisma,&nbsp;
and the blue stentorin in Stentor coeruleus.&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:19.040
These pigments are used as a toxin against&nbsp;
predators. But they can make their own organism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:25.440
sensitive to light to a dangerous degree,&nbsp;
providing excellent motivation to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.680
said light. And these photophobic responses&nbsp;
may also help the blepharisma and stentors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.560
avoid predators with better vision than their own.&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:43.040
These photosensitive pigments are like nature’s&nbsp;
way of unlocking the foundations of vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:50.240
They are not themselves a specialized structure.&nbsp;
To see an example of a specialized structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:57.680
let’s look at that bright red eyespot on the&nbsp;
euglena. Also called a stigma, the eyespot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:05.440
is not actually an eye. It’s more like&nbsp;
a sunglass for the true photoreceptor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.960
That structure is located close to the euglena’s&nbsp;
flagella, and it’s made up of around 50 layers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:20.320
stacked membranes that hold hexagonal arrangements&nbsp;
of roughly a million photoreceptor proteins.&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:26.800
The stigma shades the photoreceptor,&nbsp;
but just on one side of the euglena.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:33.360
And as the microbe rotates and different&nbsp;
wavelengths of light filter through and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:40.480
get shaded by the stigma, the euglena is actually&nbsp;
getting information on where it can find light.&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:51.520
As we shift from unicellular to multicellular&nbsp;
organisms, we start to see what microscopic eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.040
look like. One of the important evolutionary&nbsp;
developments was the formation of a cup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.840
shape in these eye-like structures,&nbsp;
which helps provide spatial information.&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:09.440
Some of these animal eyes are mysterious,&nbsp;
like those beady little buttons atop many a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.360
tardigrade’s heads. Sure, scientists have&nbsp;
documented that many tardigrade species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:22.480
have what they call “inverse pigmented eye-cups,”&nbsp;
but what do they see? Well, we do not know.&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:29.120
Others, like the planarian, have a pigmented&nbsp;
eye-cup connected to photoreceptor cells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:36.080
which actually provides enough similarities to our&nbsp;
own eyes to make them useful for medical studies.&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:43.280
And as the animals get larger, so does their&nbsp;
potential for complex visual systems. Similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:50.320
to many insects, Daphnia have compound eyes that&nbsp;
are made up of units called ommatidia, which focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:58.000
light onto photoreceptor cells to form images.
The larger the eye, the more information the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:04.720
daphnia and other animals with these kinds&nbsp;
of image-forming eyes can get. But size and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:11.840
complexity come with trade-offs. Sure, these eyes&nbsp;
help the animal navigate and find food. But these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:18.720
are costly organs to build and maintain. One&nbsp;
study found that the retina in a blowfly’s eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:27.200
took up 10% of its resting metabolic rate.
Eyes and these analogous structures have evolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:35.200
in response to what we need to process from our&nbsp;
surroundings. Needs inform evolution, which in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:44.080
turn, informs new needs. And light can provide for&nbsp;
those needs, but it can also pose its own dangers.&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:50.560
In The Origin of Species, Charles Darwin&nbsp;
famously describes the eye as an organ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:58.320
of “extreme perfection.” He introduces it as an&nbsp;
example of biological complexity that may at first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.680
pose some consternation to those&nbsp;
considering natural selection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:11.440
After all, how could something so remarkable be&nbsp;
made through iterations and iterations of tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:19.120
gradual changes? But in Darwin’s search through&nbsp;
natural history, the case for stacking gradual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:27.840
change upon gradual change seems less improbable,&nbsp;
and science has continued to support his theory.&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.160
With that said, it is tempting to get caught&nbsp;
up in this story that has been cultivated over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:42.000
eons and relate it entirely to the structures&nbsp;
that make up our own eyes. That was our entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.480
point into this discussion after all. But one of&nbsp;
the most remarkable aspects of evolution is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:57.680
understanding that it is not something that was&nbsp;
leading to us. Evolution has led to a whole world.&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:05.600
All of these remarkable structures and chemicals&nbsp;
have their own unique properties that suit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:13.280
organisms they reside in, allowing creatures&nbsp;
that, yes, may be simpler than ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:21.280
to use systems that are not just good enough&nbsp;
for their survival, they are ideal for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:29.760
survival. These organisms are thus able to survive&nbsp;
in ways that you and I could never be capable of.&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:39.760
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

